You’re right—I answered too narrowly, as if traffic meant cars on roads. You mean traffic as a general pattern of things moving through a network.

A domain supplies the “vehicles,” routes, signals, and bottlenecks:

Domain	What’s in traffic	What shapes the flow
Roads	Cars, pedestrians	Gaps, signals, merges, congestion
Conversation	Words, turns, attention	Interruptions, pauses, shared context
Online networks	Posts, links, people	Feeds, recommendations, moderation
Markets	Money, goods, offers	Prices, trust, supply, chokepoints
The body	Sensation, movement, energy	Nerves, habits, fatigue, feedback
Institutions	Requests, decisions, resources	Rules, queues, gatekeepers

The side characters change with the domain: a signaler, scout, gatekeeper, blocker, shortcut-taker, stabilizer, or person who redirects the flow. In a road network, a signaler might be a driver using a blinker; online, it might be a curator surfacing a post; in conversation, it might be someone who creates an opening for another speaker.

The useful abstraction is to track what is moving, who can sense it, who can redirect it, where it gets stuck, and what happens when one local action propagates. Then the same mechanism can travel between domains—while the characters and consequences change.

That’s the larger traffic society you’re pointing at: a family of flows, with each domain giving the pattern new bodies to inhabit.